`apps/designcraft-web/src/web.rs::mime_for` maps the `designcraft` extension to `application/json`, and the download …

What happens 
Fonts installed in ~/Library/Fonts (macOS) or ~/.local/share/fonts and ~/.fonts (Linux) are never used for text. Any family that isn't bundled is reported as missing by font.list and rendered and exported in Source Sans 3, even though the font files are installed and their name tables are correct (typographic family and subfamily names, ID 16/17, match the IDML exactly).

Steps to reproduce (CLI)
1. Install any font that isn't bundled, e.g. Bebas Neue, in ~/.local/share/fonts (Linux) or ~/Library/Fonts (macOS).
2. Run this script with designcraft-cli script test.txt:
3. file.new {"preset":"Letter"}
4. frame.create {"rect":[36,36,500,100],"content":"text","text":"Hello"}
5. edit.selectAll {}
6. type.char {"attrs":{"fontFamily":"Bebas Neue","fontStyle":"Regular"}}
7. font.list {}
8. file.exportPdf {"path":"/tmp/fonttest.pdf"}
9. font.list reports Bebas Neue as missing, and the PDF embeds only SourceSans3-Regular.

The same happens in the desktop app when exporting an IDML whose fonts are all installed: the PDF uses Source Sans 3 for all text.

Expected 
Installed fonts are found by family/style name and used for layout and PDF export, as in InDesign.

Notes 
In the v0.2.1 tag, FontDb::load_system_fonts is documented as "not called by default", and the system scan only appears to run for fallback glyphs. main seems to catalog installed fonts on the first family lookup, so this may already be fixed; a release with that change would solve it. Also tried a "Document Fonts" folder next to the IDML: no change in 0.2.1.

## Summary
`type.char` with a caret (an empty text selection) in a story that already has text changes nothing and returns success. Text typed afterwards keeps the old formatting. In an **empty** story the same call works: it sets the typing format and later text gets it.

This is the stub in `format_chars` (`crates/engine/src/cmd/text.rs`, the caret branch):

```rust
if r.is_empty() {
    // Caret: change the typing format (the empty run at the caret).
    let pos = r.start;
    if st.is_empty() {
        st.chars[0].format.over.merge(&a);
        st.rev += 1;
    } else {
        let _ = pos;   // <- non-empty story: nothing happens
    }
    continue;
}
```

## Why it shows up often
`frame.create` with `content: "text"` leaves the caret at the end of the new text (`caret` defaults to true), so a natural agent sequence hits it every time:

`frame.create {text: "Headline"}` → `type.char {size: 36}` → `null`, and the headline stays at the default size.

The same applies to a user who clicks into text, changes the size in the Character panel and types.

## Repro (designcraft-cli 0.4.0, headless MCP; same code on main at 4e0e394)
```json
{"tool":"new_document","args":{"width":612,"height":792}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[54,54,558,300],"content":"text","text":"small "}}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"type.char","params":{"size":48}}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"text.insert","params":{"text":"BIG?"}}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[54,320,558,560],"content":"text","text":""}}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"type.char","params":{"size":48}}}
{"tool":"execute","args":{"command":"text.insert","params":{"text":"empty story BIG?"}}}
{"tool":"export_png","args":{"path":"out.png","scale":1}}
```
Both `type.char` calls return `null`.

| | Result |
|---|---|
| Frame 2 (empty story) | "empty story BIG?" at 48 pt, as expected |
| Frame 1 (story with text) | "small BIG?" entirely …

A placed PDF or AI file carries its crop choice in IDML as a `<PDFAttribute>` element on the placed graphic (InDesign's own IDML for these placements has `PDFCrop="CropMedia"`). `file.exportIdml` writes no `PDFAttribute`, so when InDesign opens the file it applies its own default crop (the placement reports `CROP_CONTENT_VISIBLE_LAYERS`) and the graphic's scale and position change.

**Repro** (designcraft-cli 0.5.0, main @ 92649ff)
```
file.new {"width": 612, "height": 792, "pages": 1}
frame.create {"rect": [36, 200, 300, 400], "content": "graphic"}
file.place {"path": "any.pdf", "frame": "$1.id", "pdfCrop": "media"}
file.exportIdml {"path": "out.idml"}
```
Unzip `out.idml`: no file contains `PDFAttribute` or `PDFCrop`. `crates/idml` has no reference to either name, so import presumably ignores the attribute too (not separately measured).

**Measured in InDesign 21.6 (Windows)**
- InDesign IDML -> DesignCraft -> `file.exportIdml` -> InDesign, on a 2-page sports program: 18 of 18 placed PDF/AI lost their crop; 9 of 16 linked files changed scale, one from 83.7% to 299.18%.
- Layout authored in DesignCraft: a mascot PDF placed with `pdfCrop: "media"` measured 48.2% in DesignCraft and 81.44% after opening the IDML in InDesign.

**Suggestion:** write `<PDFAttribute PageNumber=... PDFCrop=... TransparentBackground=...>` on export from the frame's crop setting (CropMedia, CropTrim, CropBleed, CropArt, CropContent...), and read it on import.

**Version:** designcraft-cli 0.7.1 (also 0.6.0), Windows 11
**Type:** bug / missing feature. Related: #389 (IDML import ignores drop shadow from object styles), which is likely the same gap seen from the import side.

### Repro
```
file.new {"width":2304,"height":1440,"pages":1}
frame.create {"rect":[100,100,600,500],"content":"unassigned"}
object.fill {"swatch":"[Paper]","ids":["$1.id"]}
object.dropShadow {"on":true,"distance":0,"size":12,"opacity":0.28,"ids":["$1.id"]}
selection.set {"ids":["$1.id"]}
style.object.create {"name":"Photo","fromSelection":true}
frame.create {"rect":[700,100,1200,500],"content":"unassigned"}
style.object.apply {"name":"Photo","ids":["$6.id"]}
document.inspect {}
```

**Actual:** in the saved `document.json`, `styles.object[name=Photo]` only has `fill` and `stroke`. The second frame gets the fill but no shadow. Every shadow has to be set per object and can't be changed centrally. `style.object.create` only accepts `{name, fromSelection, fill, paragraphStyle}`.
**Expected:** like InDesign object styles, the style carries effects.

### Acceptance criteria
- `style.object.create {"fromSelection":true}` stores the selection's `effects` (at least `dropShadow`, ideally `feather`, `innerShadow`, `outerGlow` too, whatever `object.*` effect commands exist).
- `style.object.apply` sets those effects on the target, and items keep following the style until they get local overrides (same model as fill/stroke).
- Effects can be set on a style without a selection, e.g. `style.object.edit {"name":"Photo","dropShadow":{...}}`, and editing the style updates all items using it.
- IDML export writes them to the ObjectStyle, and IDML import reads them (closes #389).

### Context
I rebuilt a 46-page portfolio with ~100 drop-shadowed photo frames. Without this, changing the house shadow means touching every frame.

**What happens**
Preferences OK takes any number for the Story Editor font size. A value that is not finite (`"nan"`, `"inf"`, or a number too large for an `f32` such as `1e39`) is saved to `ui.json` as `null`. On the next launch the file can't be read, nothing is reported and the app starts with the default UI settings. When it quits it writes those defaults over the file, so saved workspaces, custom shortcuts and the rest of the UI state are gone.

**Steps** (main e862e0a, `designcraft --control 7979`, with `DESIGNCRAFT_CONFIG_DIR` set to an empty directory)

1. Save something to lose, then quit:
```
{"id":1,"method":"ui.menu.invoke","params":{"command":"window.newWorkspace","params":{"name":"Mine"}}}
{"id":2,"method":"ui.menu.invoke","params":{"command":"window.setShortcut","params":{"id":"app.palette","shortcut":"Cmd+Alt+Shift+9"}}}
{"id":3,"method":"app.quit"}
```
`ui.json` now has the workspace, the shortcut and `"storyEditorSize": 14.0`.

2. Start again, confirm Preferences with the bad size, then quit:
```
{"id":1,"method":"ui.menu.invoke","params":{"command":"app.preferences"}}
{"id":2,"method":"ui.dialog.set","params":{"field":"storyEditorSize","value":"nan"}}
{"id":3,"method":"ui.dialog.confirm"}
{"id":4,"method":"app.quit"}
```
The confirm answers ok. `ui.json` now has `"storyEditorSize": null`; the workspace and the shortcut are still in it.

3. Start again. `ui.inspect` shows `"customWorkspaces": []`, `"shortcuts": {}` and `"storyEditorSize": 14.0`, and nothing about the file is logged. After `app.quit`, `ui.json` holds the defaults.

`"inf"` and the number `1e39` give the same `null`.

**Cause**
`dialogs::confirm` stores `d.n("storyEditorSize").unwrap_or(14.0) as f32` as it comes (`crates/ui-egui/src/dialogs.rs:2012`). `save_prefs` writes the UI state with serde_json, which turns a float that is not finite into `null`. `load_prefs` (`apps/designcraft/src/main.rs:103`) can't read an `f32` from `null`, drops the whole file without a message and keeps th…

**What happens**
With Show Baseline Grid on, a document whose baseline grid starts far below zero makes the next frame draw grid lines without end. The window stops responding and memory grows by more than 1 GiB a second until something kills the process. Anything not yet saved or written to recovery is lost, in every open document.

**Steps** (main e862e0a, `designcraft --control 7979`)
```
{"id":1,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"file.new","params":{}}}
{"id":2,"method":"ui.set","params":{"baselineGrid":true}}
{"id":3,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"document.preferences","params":{"baselineGrid":{"start":-1e17}}}}
{"id":4,"method":"ui.inspect"}
```
Request 3 answers ok. Request 4 gets no reply, and resident memory goes from 220 MiB to 1.6 GiB in about a second. I stopped the process there.

Opening a file does the same while Show Baseline Grid is on. Both of these open without a warning and then hang on the next frame:
- a `.designcraft` file saved with that start,
- an IDML package with `BaselineStart="-100000000000000000"` in `Resources/Preferences.xml`.

A start of `-1e9` is enough to pass 1.5 GiB within a second. With `-1e7` the app stays up, at 40 to 60 ms a frame.

**Cause**
`draw_guides` walks the grid from its start, one line per step (`crates/ui-egui/src/canvas.rs:615-622`):
```rust
let mut y = g.start;
while y < p.height {
    painter.line_segment(…);
    y += g.increment.max(1.0);
}
```
Nothing bounds the number of lines, and the lines above the page are drawn too. The start is taken as it comes by `document.preferences` (`crates/engine/src/cmd/prefs.rs`), by the file loader and by the IDML import (`grid_prefs`, `crates/idml/src/import.rs:601`). Only the Preferences dialog keeps it at 0 or above (`crates/ui-egui/src/dialogs.rs:2043`). The loop runs on the UI thread while painting, so the panic guard has no part in it.

**Expected**
Drawing the baseline grid costs no more than the lines that fall on the page, whatever start a…

**What happens**
A document with two stories ends the app on the next render: one story holds a running header variable and is the destination of a cross-reference, the other holds that cross-reference. The process aborts with `thread 'main' has overflowed its stack`. That is not a panic, so the panic guard can't catch it, and anything not yet saved or written to recovery is lost, in every open document.

**Steps** (main e862e0a, `designcraft --control 7979`)
```
{"id":1,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"file.new","params":{"pages":1,"facingPages":false}}}
{"id":2,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[72,72,400,120],"content":"text","text":"Chapter one"}}}
{"id":3,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[72,200,400,300],"content":"text","text":"See ."}}}
{"id":4,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"text.select","params":{"story":8,"anchor":0,"focus":0}}}
{"id":5,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"variables.insert","params":{"name":"Running Header"}}}
{"id":6,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"text.select","params":{"story":10,"anchor":4,"focus":4}}}
{"id":7,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"xref.insert","params":{"story":8,"para":0}}}
```
Requests 2 and 3 answer with stories 8 and 10. Request 7 answers ok, then the app exits:
```
thread 'main' (…) has overflowed its stack
fatal runtime error: stack overflow, aborting
```
The same commands through `designcraft-cli run … --export out.png` end the same way (exit status 134). Either half alone renders: only the variable, or only the cross-reference.

Nothing here is unusual input. It is the default Running Header variable and the default cross-reference format, the commands behind Type › Text Variables › Insert Variable and Type › Hyperlinks & Cross-References › Insert Cross-Reference….

**Cause**
`Cache::get` asks for the running-header index when a story has a running header variab…

**What happens**
The Table panel's Header Rows and Footer Rows fields pass whatever number is typed to `table.options`, which adds that many rows to the table. Nothing limits it. With 200,000 the app stops responding, memory passes 2 GiB within about two seconds and keeps growing; larger numbers go the same way faster. Anything not yet saved or written to recovery is lost.

**Steps** (main e862e0a, `designcraft --control 7979`)
```
{"id":1,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"file.new","params":{}}}
{"id":2,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[72,72,500,400],"content":"text","text":""}}}
{"id":3,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"text.select","params":{"story":9,"anchor":0,"focus":0}}}
{"id":4,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"table.insert","params":{"rows":2,"cols":2}}}
{"id":5,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"table.options","params":{"headerRows":200000}}}
{"id":6,"method":"ui.inspect"}
```
Request 2 answers with story 9. Request 5 answers after about half a second, with memory at 1.7 GiB instead of 200 MiB. Request 6 gets no reply and memory keeps growing; I stopped the process at 2 GiB. With `headerRows: 1000000000` request 5 itself never answers.

It doesn't take the control channel. With the caret in a table, the Header Rows field of the Table panel sends the same command: typing 300 there and pressing Return gives a table of 301 rows, 300 of them header rows.

**Cause**
`table.options` adds rows until the table holds the requested header and footer rows plus one body row (`crates/engine/src/cmd/table.rs:934-945`):
```rust
