let f = p.get("footerRows").and_then(Value::as_u64).map(|v| v as usize);
…
let need = h + f + 1;
if t.nrows() < need {
    let at = t.nrows();
    t.insert_rows(at, need - at);
}
```
Neither count has an upper bound, and the sum is unchecked. The other table commands have limits: …

**What happens**
The Size of a drop shadow, inner shadow or outer glow has no upper limit. With 10,000 pt the next canvas render needs gigabytes: memory passes 3 GiB within half a second and keeps growing, and the app stops responding. Anything not yet saved or written to recovery is lost.

**Steps** (main 11a51d5b, `designcraft --control 7979`)
```
{"id":1,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"file.new","params":{}}}
{"id":2,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"frame.create","params":{"rect":[150,150,350,350]}}}
{"id":3,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"object.dropShadow","params":{"on":true,"size":10000}}}
{"id":4,"method":"ui.inspect"}
```
Request 3 answers ok. Request 4 gets no reply, and resident memory goes from 200 MiB to over 3 GiB in about half a second. I stopped the process there.

It doesn't take the control channel. With the frame selected, typing 10000 into the Size field of the Drop Shadow section in the Effects panel and pressing Return does the same. `object.innerShadow` and `object.outerGlow` with `size: 10000` do too. 1,000 pt and 3,000 pt still work; 3,000 pt costs about 270 MiB.

